   671	- **The one-ahead state, and the merge (reading disclosed)** — each segment keeps `delta_counts` (the calls since its state at `n_queries − 1`; canonical, inside its counts). With N = the segment's `n_queries` and M = the highest `n_queries` its self-verifying rows' stamps carry: N == M → its counts digest must be the last row's stamped state (the empty state when M = 0); N == M + 1 → ONE query ahead (killed between the ledger write and the row write) → `counts − delta_counts` must be the last row's stamped state, so the prefix every published row rests on is bound and only the lost attempt's calls are not; anything else (behind, or two ahead) is refused. "Admitting ONLY a single one-query-ahead state at reopen … and exact equality at merge" is read PER SEGMENT and for the run's LAST state: at a reopen any segment may be one ahead; at a merge (`exact`) a shard's LAST invocation must be exact (it completed and wrote the run summary), while an earlier crashed segment stays one ahead forever (its lost row is regenerated by the resume, in a later segment) and is admitted only if every call shape of its lost attempt was executed again by a later segment of the same shard (the resume re-ran that query at the same declared batching) — so a one-ahead state can neither HIDE a call (its prefix is bound) nor INVENT a call shape (e.g. a phantom full-size call that would saturate a maximum). Literal "exact equality at merge" for every segment would refuse every crash-and-resume shard, which D-R2-6 admits. Intermediate stamps (n < M) are not bound to stored states (the ledger keeps the final state only, as in Codex's design); a row whose own digest fails is ignored here (a resume regenerates it, the merge refuses it); regenerating the LAST row of an earlier segment leaves that segment one ahead (admitted, bound), regenerating its last two leaves it two ahead (refused: restart).
